    "finding": "Instruction-tuned models refuse a harmful request in its native script and answer the same request written in Latin letters. The usual reading is that safety training is tied to the script. A second reading is that the model cannot read the rewritten text. Neither survives our measurements. We test recovery two ways, and neither lets the model produce a response that could be scored as a refusal. Asked to rewrite ITRANS as Devanagari, five of six models score above 97% character similarity. Shown the rewritten request and four candidate meanings in the native script, models pick the right one in 42% to 97.5% of items, read from the output distribution. Recovery exceeds engagement in every interpretable cell, by 22 to 80 percentage points. The model neither refuses nor complies: scoring every on-topic response for harmful content, specific usable material appears in at most 4% of items in any cell, and the largest outcome for two of the three rewritings is off-topic text. Making the recovery explicit restores refusal. Asking the model to rewrite the request and then answer its own rewriting improves refusal in eight of ten cells with no retraining, closing up to 79% of the gap to the native-script ceiling, and how much it gains tracks how well the model decoded (r = 0.75).",
